--- PARITY (web/src/features/dashboard/widgets/UptimeMonitorWidget.tsx) ---
Registry uptime-monitor, category system, 2x2 / 1x2 / 4x40 (cols x rows), slug UptimeMonitorWidget.
Data via useSystemHealth -> GET /system/health (generated op get_api_v1_system_health; resolves to
/system/health in the generated endpoint table, asserted by a test). No HTTP in the view; flows through the
shared P1/S8 IUptimeMonitorSource -> UptimeMonitorViewModel (cache-then-network).
Composition: ServiceKeys [database, mqtt, tesla_api, fleet_telemetry]; statusVariant ok/healthy->success,
degraded->warning, else->danger (dot + badge tone). Overall = status ?? 'unknown'; overall badge
healthy->"All OK" else raw status. Service label = t(`widget.uptime.${key}`, TitleCase(key)) (faithful C#
port of the JS replace(/_/g,' ').replace(/\b\w/g, upper) fallback); service badge "OK" for healthy else raw
status. isCompact (cols==1 && rows==1) -> centred healthyCount/total; else per-service rows; isTall (rows>=2)
-> DB Size / Tables footer (each ?? em-dash; the live /system/health body omits database_size/table_count, so
the footer shows em-dash exactly as the web's ?? '—'). data null/non-object -> EmptyState "No system health
data". States loading/loaded/empty/error/stale/offline all render.
i18n: the 6 source keys (title, overall, allOk, dbSize, tables, noData) are already present in
Strings/{en,he,ar}/Resources.resw + apps/shared/i18n/catalog/{en,he,ar}.json; service labels use computed
fallbacks (the web has no catalog keys for them either). All strings resolve through the P1/S10 ILocalizer
facade — no English literals in native code (check-placeholders clean).
Accessibility: Narrator names on overall row, each service row, compact metric, footer rows, and the refresh
button; status dots are AccessibilityView.Raw; loading is a polite LiveRegion. System font scale flows via
WinUI effective-pixel sizing; no custom animation so reduced-motion is inherently honoured.
Telemetry: view.opened slug=UptimeMonitorWidget (PII-safe; never a status/error/db-size).
New tests: 67 cases (parse adapter incl. snake_case + camelCase + non-object; statusVariant/IsHealthy/
TitleCase ports; size flags; projection overall/services/healthy-count/footer/absent; full view-model state
matrix loading/loaded/empty/error/stale/offline + size reproject + i18n + PropertyChanged; source single-call
+ op-id; registry metadata/bounds/clamp/op-id-resolves; diagnostics).

--- PRE-EXISTING BLOCKER FIXED (required for a green solution build) ---
The integration base (HEAD 46d9aedd8) did not compile: SafetyFeaturesWidget.Model.cs AND
SafetyHistoryWidget.Model.cs BOTH declared SafetyValue / SafetyValueKind / SafetyEnumField / SafetySnapshot in
namespace TeslaSync.App.DashboardWidgets (CS0101 duplicate definition). That fails MarkupCompilePass1 -> no
LocalAssembly -> cascading XAML WMC0001/WMC9999 across ComponentGallery.xaml / Styles.xaml / ShellWindow.xaml,
so the whole TeslaSync.App project (and thus the gate) was RED before this surface was touched. The required
capability (WinUI 3 + .NET 10) is present and proven by a clean Release build below. Surgical fix: renamed the
four colliding types in the SafetyHistoryWidget feature (the side with fewer references, all confined to its
own 4 files + its test, 0 external referencers) to SafetyHistory{Value,ValueKind,EnumField,Snapshot}. The
SafetyHistoryWidget view class stays in the flat namespace (no external reference breakage). SafetyHistory +
SafetyFeatures tests remain green (210/210 for the three widgets together; 5450/5450 overall). Also added the
three UptimeMonitorWidget logic files to TeslaSync.App.Tests.csproj <Compile> (every widget is wired this way
so its UI-thread-free logic is unit-tested without the WinUI assembly).

